{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"security_deposits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Security Deposit Act transfer and penalties","requirement":"Landlord must transfer security deposit and accrued interest to new owner; new owner responsible for investment, notices, interest, and return. Willfully withholding deposit of tenant receiving state/federal financial assistance may incur civil penalty of $500-$2,000 per offense.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential rental security deposits.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 per offense for withholding deposit of tenant receiving financial assistance; criminal fine of $200 or imprisonment up to 30 days for unlawful use of deposits.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-21","quoted_span":"Any landlord who willfully and intentionally withholds a security deposit made by or on behalf of a tenant who has received financial assistance through any Stat e or federal program, including welfare or rental assistance , may be penalized. The landlord may be liable for a civil penalty of not less than $500 or not more than $2,000 for each offense.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings rent control exemption","requirement":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings are exempt from local rent control ordinances for 30 years after completion of construction. Subsidized rents may also be exempt.","key_value":"30 years","coverage_conditions":"Newly constructed multiple dwellings.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":"construction_date","min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"newly constructed multiple dwellings"},"exemptions":"Rents subsidized by governmental funding may also be exempt from local rent control ordinances.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-84.5","quoted_span":"Under the Newly Constructed Multiple Dwellings Law ( N.J.S.A. 2A:42 -84.5), newly constructed multiple dwellings shall be exempt from any local rent control ordinances for a period of 30 years following completion of construction of the building.","interaction":"Exempts newly constructed multiple dwellings from local rent control ordinances.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act rent increase unconscionability and notice","requirement":"A landlord may not raise rent mid-lease; must terminate old lease, offer new lease with increased rent, and give proper written notice. Rent increases must not be unconscionable and must comply with municipal rent control ordinances.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential tenants covered by the Anti-Eviction Act.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"The increase in rent must not be unconscionable; it must not be so unreasonable as to shock the conscience of a fair and honest person and must comply with any municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","interaction":"Rent increases must comply with municipal ordinances governing rent increases.","confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Condominium conversion rent increase restriction","requirement":"If a building is converted to condominium or cooperative ownership, rents may not be increased to cover costs resulting solely from the conversion. Increases for new services or amenities are allowed.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All tenants in buildings converted to condominium, cooperative, or fee simple ownership.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":"Increases to cover cost of new services or amenities are allowed.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31","quoted_span":"If a building is converted to a condominium or cooperative form of ownership, or to fee simple ownership of units, rents may not be increased to cover cos ts resulting solely from the conversion (N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.31).","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act good cause requirement","requirement":"A residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease only for good cause, proven in court. Except for nonpayment of rent, each cause must be described in a written notice, and a Notice to Quit is required.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All residential tenants, unless exempt.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":null},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No residential landlord may evict or fail to renew a lease, whether it is a written or an oral lease without good cause. The landlord must be able to prove in court that he has grounds for an eviction.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-02","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"screening_restrictions","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"LAD source of lawful income protection","requirement":"Landlords may not discriminate in rental housing based on source of lawful income, among other protected classes. Exemptions include owner-occupied two-family dwellings and certain single-sex or age-restricted housing.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All landlord-tenant relationships, except two-family owner-occupied dwellings, rooms in owner-occupied single home, single-sex residences, and age-restricted housing for familial status.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"exemptions for owner-occupied two-family, owner-occupied single home rooms, single-sex, age-restricted"},"exemptions":"Two-family owner-occupied dwellings; rooms in owner- or resident-occupied single home; single-sex residences (YMCA, age-restricted housing) for familial status.","penalty":"Up to $10,000 for a first offense.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 10:5-12","quoted_span":"The New Jersey Law Against Discrimination (LAD), N.J.S.A. 10:5-12(g) to -(h), prohibits discrimination when selling or renting property and requires equal treatment in the sale or rental of housing regardless of race, creed, color, national origin, ancestry, sex, marital status, civil union status, domestic partnership status, familial status, affectional or sexual orientation, gender  identity or expression, mental and physical disability, nationality, or source of lawful income.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
